  "worked_solution": "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/3f85af53794fa2f61a353f2e088cc1cecc9fc04f7453b97aa0ca808475e40035.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the observer and the launch point,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the drone and the launch point, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the observer and the drone.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the drone altitude after $4$ seconds. Since its velocity is constant, we get \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot t=40(4)=160\\text{\u00a0ft}\n$$\n\nWe can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {120}^{2}+{160}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 40\\,000 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =200\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=40\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$\n\n- the distance between the observer and the launch pad remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(120)(0)+2(160)(40) & =2(200)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 12\\,800 & =400\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the drone and the observer increases at a rate of $32\\,\\text{ft/s}$ after $4$ seconds.",
